@blkslymn Nope for $AKAM. But if you look at what’s needed probably CPUs/Memory #1.

So your $ARM / $INTC / $AMD thematically (AMD probably higher beta to this company in specific). Then your Samsung / $MU / SK Hynix are your obvious memory beneficiaries.

Component procurement is done through $JBL, which potentially benefits their own supply chain if they’re doing networking too.

@Omer231ew Timing the ramp-up is the most important part. Get ahead of yourself and you lose most of those gains; be too late and the re-rating will already be behind you.

AAOI should, all things considered, make it back to its ATH and then some, if they can find a proper way to finance their growth other than constant ATM offerings.

SIVE is an H2 2027 CPO story; it's just that retail got ahead of itself.

@ToppsGallery NVDA is a high volume stock. If you buy it, you’ll likely buy right at the stock price it shows.

If you buy a stock that nobody usually buys with low volume, you might place a buy at $100. But it actually gets filled at $103. 

Just simple terms, low volume stocks avoid market buy. Limit only.
